 example text labels that have been hard  coded in the image a paper that looks at  all of that in depth called tcaf and  that tries to measure that and quantify  that in some way as linked below it's a  really interesting and well-written  paper that gives you a lot of new ways  to view at what a network does  internally and how we can actually put  that to numbers  we then had two questions about vector  quantized variation and auto encoders  while those don't sound that exciting  remember that they enable for a great  deal of things like dali  in principle a vq vae is an auto encoder  where the latent space is quantized that  is you encode some input then round that  latent code to the closest codebook  value and then decode that discretized  value to obtain the original input again  this learnt discrete representation then  works a bit better for some tasks  because you can more easily use for  example transformers on it that are  built for discrete inputs also some  things in the real world like words or  handwritten digits are just discrete so  it comes more naturally to express them  through a discrete representation now  this code book that we are using to  discretize the encoder output is also  learned during training which means that  for the lust function we now have the  original reconstruction laws just as for  a normal boring autoencoder  and two parts to regularize the latent  space to better fit the values of the  codebook while also moving the used code  book items closer to the samples  if we now just drop that vector  quantization part namely those two extra  losses that adapt the code book to the  latent codes and the latent codes to the  code book all that's left is the  autoencoder reconstruction loss and  without that discretization well we then  just have a plain old auto encoder and  that also means that we no longer get  the properties similar to a variational  autoencoder namely a nice and tidy  latent through using this code book  similar to that question before look at  let's what happens if we make the code  book of a vq vae infinitely large  for the loss functions remember that we  take our encoded image and then quantize  that to the nearest codebook values  before we decode that now there are two  possible interpretations of that  questions that largely depend on the  initial distribution of the let's see  one dimensional here code book and the  initial distribution of the latent first  of all if we say that an infinite code  book would actually mean a code book  with all possible code words in it then  we would have all the possible numbers  our encoder could come up with in there  if that's the case then both the losses  would become 0. if every latent part is  actually in the code book then we would  not need to move our codebook closer to  the encoder output or move the output of  the encoder closer to the codebook and  as a result these terms are always zero  and we just have a regular old boring  autoencoder and that is what i was  actually going for with this question  but as the comments pointed out that's  not the only possible interpretation for  the other possible interpretation let's  say that our infinite code book has a  certain distribution for example having  numbers from -1 to 1. if our encoder  luckily falls into that range and we  stay there great we are not going to  have any quantization related losses  that are non-zero but if we produce an  encoding that gets out of that range  things get a little bit hairy if we have  a value outside that range say smaller  than -1 we move that value that comes  out of the encoder towards -1 by moving  the code book entry with -1 towards that  actual value however we just move that  one entry from the boundary there not  touching any of the other values in the  code book for now as the code book is  only adapted for those code words that  are actually used so now we have code  book that has some values being  quantized and some not being quantized  and over the iterations that will just  spread out and we for example end up  with something like this we have a core  dense region between -1 and 1 where  quantization doesn't do anything because  all the values are in that code book and  then there is a region where we just  regularly discretize  so this will eventually act like a  regular auto encoder for anything inside  -1 and 1  and like a regular vq vae outside of  that which is somewhat weird can you  come up with a useful scenario for that  or do you even think that we overlooked  something and we get an entirely  different behavior if so hit us up in  the comments because for now that seems  more like a pile of rubbish to me next  up is a fantastic question yay thank you  for that we wanted to know whether one  could use pearson's correlation  coefficient to actually disentangle the  latent space of a neural network well  disentangling basically means that we  want the latent of our neural network to  be nice and tidy for example that an  auto encoder of handwritten digits is  actually split into different  characteristics like having one variable  on the latent express thickness and the  other variable in the latent expressing  the number which is actually displayed  sadly an unregularized latent space  isn't that cleaned up and these  characteristics are all over the place  maybe we want the distribution where  this latent space is nicely split into  one variable having thickness and the  other one containing the digits all nice  and tidy but in truth they are actually  looking like this instead with one  variable having both characteristics in  them a little bit  and that's just one possible way but  this is something we actually do not  want for disentangled representation  what's important is that these variables  are somewhat correlated in the latent  space now meaning that for example when  we have some information about the  x-axis we also get some information  about the y-axis as well 